**Example (Explicit root equations and a missing transitive relation)** · `ex:nrz:project-review-nested-roots`

S3's `solving/real/single_produce.rs::radical_sum` explicitly proves `s²=D` after adjoining the positive square root and before adjoining two odd roots. For `t³+p*t+q`, the outer equations are `u³=-q/2+s` and `v³=-q/2-s`; their product must establish `(u*v)³=(-p/3)³` before `OddPowerReflection` establishes `u*v=-p/3`. The existing `x³+x+1` fixture (`testing/solve_blind/real_oracle.rs`, public Real conformance) has `p=1`, `q=1`, `D=31/108`. In ordered formal variables `u,v,s`, reducing `u³*v³+1/27` by `u³+1/2-s` then `v³+1/2+s` leaves `31/108-s²`. Neither supplied leading monomial divides it; adding `s²-31/108` gives zero.

Diagnostic 5 confirms exactly those remainders with the existing Form reducer in a disposable test `solving::elementary::reduction::review_example::nested_root_remainder`. It uses formal slots for the fixture's selected-root atoms and independently specifies the expected remainder; it is an arithmetic reproduction, not a new whole-solver acceptance test. Setup plus assertions charge 915 coefficient operations and 22,526 Work; these totals are not a comparison between proposed algorithms.

The cause here is missing relation closure, not an unfortunate order among the same two relations. `solving/elementary/selected.rs::root_relations` scans atoms in the initial Form once; an inner `s` introduced while evaluating outer equations does not trigger recursive collection. The explicit checked equality supplies it through `Evaluation::relations`. Complete transitive collection alone closes this example with the existing reducer and is the smallest justified repair; it does not require a new Gröbner implementation or certificate format.

S4's `solving/family/algebraic.rs::roots` adjoins a cubic root, divides it out monically, adjoins a quotient root and takes the last linear root. After shifting each returned root it calls `equation` with the shift added back. That helper Horner-evaluates the original depressed coefficient polynomial and emits a checked `DefinedAndEqual` equality to zero through `solving/radicals/proof.rs::identity`. In `tests/family_solver.rs::request(4)`, each of the three selected values gets the equation `z³+c*z-r=0`. These are independently checked consequences of the quotient tower, not axioms. They expose original-polynomial relations for downstream scalar membership/factor reduction, whose one-level collection and finite order otherwise need not recover them from nested quotient equations. The record has not measured removal of every S4 equality, so it does not claim a particular failing S4 remainder.

Completeness still fails for arbitrary relations even after collection. Expanding `p-q=q*(p*q-1)-p*(q²-1)` proves ideal membership, yet leading-term division of `p-q` by `p*q-1` and `q²-1` cannot start in the current graded order. This displayed algebraic counterexample is distinct from diagnostic 5. Restarting repairs some interactions (`solving/real/parameter_tests.rs::real_parameter_revisits_generators`) but creates no S-polynomials and decides no general ideal.

